 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Residual-Proxy Adaptive Selector
-An adaptive LOD selection method that uses residual proxies to recover quality over fixed threshold methods at comparable primitive counts [S2, unofficial]. It is particularly effective in scenarios where sub-pixel redundancy is exhausted, such as in aerial models tessellated at their training viewing distance [S2, unofficial]. It is chosen when near-lossless quality is required with minimal primitive count overhead [S2, unofficial].
+An adaptive LOD selection method that uses residual proxies to recover quality over fixed threshold methods at comparable primitive counts [S2, unofficial]. On the garden scene, it recovers 0.49 dB over the fixed threshold at a comparable primitive count (1.05×) [S2, unofficial]. On the city-scale model, a parameter $c=4$ renders 68% of the primitives at −0.05 dB loss [S2, unofficial]. The city aerial model exhibits a wider near-lossless window than the garden model because it is already tessellated at its training viewing distance, exhausting sub-pixel redundancy at small $c$ and leaving surplus in super-pixel collapse [S2, unofficial]. It is chosen when near-lossless quality is required with minimal primitive count overhead [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-The method improves image quality for large volumetric data by adapting the resolution to the visual importance [S1]. Performance characteristics are not explicitly quantified in the sources, but the use of an image-based metric suggests a computational overhead for quality estimation [S1]. The Residual-Proxy variant demonstrates significant quality recovery (0.49 dB) over fixed thresholds at comparable primitive counts (1.05x) on specific scenes [S2, unofficial].
+The method improves image quality for large volumetric data by adapting resolution based on an image-based quality metric [S1]. Performance characteristics are not explicitly quantified in the sources, but the use of an image-based metric implies computational overhead for quality estimation [S1]. The Residual-Proxy variant recovers 0.49 dB over fixed thresholds at 1.05× primitive count on the garden scene [S2, unofficial]. On the city-scale model, $c=4$ renders 68% of primitives at −0.05 dB loss [S2, unofficial]. Across the full ladder, the method sweeps a continuous memory–quality curve, with an operating point costing 110.6 MB against a full 1926 MB baseline (17× reduction) [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of using image-based quality metrics for LOD selection in volume rendering was proposed by Wang et al. [S1]. This approach was developed in the context of GPU-based raycasting pipelines to handle large volumetric data efficiently [S1]. The Residual-Proxy Adaptive Selector is described in the context of Budgeted-GS, a real-time large-scale Gaussian Splatting technique [S2, unofficial].
